{"author":{"id":"337a442880d7106d509ff84f2f7fef51acce8bce88b081d4f4c3f0298b7e7941"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791827701,"nonce":"slnuxcHsSfFO/Fvn9xAMgUi33Fs=","message":"Root cause: not SQLite. /apply and /job/{id}/apply-button did apply-link network work synchronously in the request: for a legacy login-wall row (group 317 on the user's DB: live, final_url id.utah.gov login) every click and every page view re-ran reresolve_legacy, walking up to 6 start URLs (aplitrak x2, cribl, jobs.utah.gov, ohiomeansjobs, mitalent, usnlx) through the per-host limiter; the walk never settles so the old row stays and the next click repeats it. The page fragment and the click walked the same hosts at once and queued behind each other on the process-wide host limiter. Evidence: fetch_log on a copy shows the console's ad-hoc walks 17:39:24-17:41:00 and 17:41:08-17:41:35 UTC; apply_click rows 6 and 7 carry request-start times 17:39:38 and 17:41:07, so the clicks hung about 80 s and 28 s. Repro on a DB copy with a concurrent resolve-stage run and a 1 s MockTransport: /apply/317 took 34.9 s, apply-button 29.3 s; faulthandler stack in ratelimit.acquire under reresolve_legacy in detail_routes.apply; all fetch_log inserts in both processes under 0.2 ms and log_click 0 ms, so no lock contention. The timing with the run was coincidence. A second, latent failure: log_click used BEGIN IMMEDIATE with busy_timeout 5000, so a write lock held by another process for over 5 s turned a click into a 500.","files":null}]}